-- rules/inquiry/ontology.rofl — the inquiry kernel's EDB contract and ledgers.
-- Perspective discipline: docs/choosing-perspectives.md. Kind taxonomy:
-- docs/inquiry-kinds.md. This file holds facts only; rules live in
-- epistemic.rofl / obligations.rofl / intents.rofl.
--
-- EDB asserted by the framer (default [main], the shared working book):
--   inquiry(Id, Kind)            -- Kind: decide|verify|explain|explore|design|monitor
--   requires(Id, Claim)          -- proof obligation of the inquiry
--   blocking(Id, Claim)          -- blocking obligation (implies requires)
--   claim(C)                     -- the claim exists in the frame
--   observable(C)                -- some experiment/scan could verify it
--   ambiguous(C)                 -- the claim's meaning is not settled
--   requires_authority(C)        -- only a human authority can settle it
--
-- EDB asserted into the observation journal [obs] by the runtime only:
--   supports[obs](Evidence, Claim)
--   refutes[obs](Evidence, Claim)
--   evidence_kind[obs](Evidence, Kind)   -- measured|human_assertion|agent_claim
--
-- Ledgers:
--   [obs]        source ledger, sole writer: runtime (evidence journal)
--   [epistemic]  derived ledger, sole writers: the rules in epistemic.rofl
--   [main]       shared working book: frame EDB + obligation/intent derivations

-- only the runtime may write the observation journal; an agent asserting
-- into [obs] surfaces as forged[audit] with no further enforcement code.
authority(obs, runtime).

-- the working book reads the epistemic ledger wholesale (explicit knowledge
-- flow). The parenthesis that stood here said "rules writing INTO [epistemic]
-- auto-bridge via their explicit heads", and that mechanism is gone: the
-- kernel used to emit `bridge_decl` for any rule whose head named a ledger and
-- whose body read another, so every rule in this layer licensed its own read
-- by existing. `crossing` no longer reads it, and the reads are declared here.
imports(main, epistemic).

-- [epistemic] READS [obs]. This is the discipline's central sentence, not an
-- incidental one: `supported[epistemic](C) :- supports[obs](E, T), ...` is the
-- worked example in docs/choosing-perspectives.md, and trust comes from WHICH
-- ledger the evidence lives in. Six rules make the hop.
imports(epistemic, obs).

-- [epistemic] READS [main]. Seven rules: `claim(C)` and `canonical(T, C)` are
-- ordinary working-book facts, and a verdict about a claim has to read the
-- claim.
imports(epistemic, main).

-- AND the gather. `agent_disputed[main](C) :- agent_state[P](C, supported),
-- agent_state[Q](C, refuted).` in perspectives.rofl reads TWO variable
-- ledgers, and `agent_attached[main](E, C) :- agent_basis[P](T, E), ...` in
-- epistemic.rofl reads a third. Agent ledgers are created by admission and
-- named at runtime, so `$var("P")` has no `authority` fact and can never be
-- the From of an import; `collects` is the only sentence available.
--
-- RESERVATION, recorded rather than hidden: [main] is also the DEFAULT ledger,
-- so this licence is broader than the two rules that need it. That is the open
-- decision in docs/books-and-permission.md 5.1, not a fault of this file. The
-- narrow repair available HERE is to give `agent_disputed` and
-- `agent_attached` a derived ledger of their own — [disputes], say — and
-- declare `collects` on THAT. It is not made because it changes what every
-- program over this layer computes.
collects(main).
